## Proof

**Yes**, each $V_\alpha \cap p^{-1}(b) \neq \varnothing$.

**Justification.** Fix any index $\alpha$. By hypothesis, the restriction
$$p\big|_{V_\alpha}: V_\alpha \to U$$
is a homeomorphism. In particular, it is **surjective** onto $U$: every point of $U$ is the image of some point of $V_\alpha$.

Since $b \in U$, surjectivity guarantees the existence of a point $e_\alpha \in V_\alpha$ such that
$$p(e_\alpha) = b.$$

By definition, $p^{-1}(b) = \{e \in E : p(e) = b\}$, so $e_\alpha \in p^{-1}(b)$. Since also $e_\alpha \in V_\alpha$, we have
$$e_\alpha \in V_\alpha \cap p^{-1}(b),$$
and therefore $V_\alpha \cap p^{-1}(b) \neq \varnothing$.

Since $\alpha$ was arbitrary, this holds for **every** sheet $V_\alpha$.

**Remark.** The covering-map hypothesis (that $p$ is a covering map) is not even needed for this particular conclusion — only the stated local condition that each $p|_{V_\alpha}$ is a homeomorphism *onto* $U$. The essential ingredient is that "homeomorphism onto $U$" entails surjectivity, so every point of $U$, including $b$, has a preimage in each sheet. Combined with the disjointness of the union, this further shows that $p^{-1}(b)$ contains exactly one point in each $V_\alpha$, i.e., the fiber over $b$ is in bijection with the index set $\{\alpha\}$.

